An asymmetric Fe–Ni dual-site catalyst embedded in N,P-co-doped carbon catalyzes the alkaline oxygen evolution reaction (OER) via the four-step associative mechanism H₂O → OH* → O* → OOH* → O₂, with the OOH* formation step (O* + H₂O → OOH*) as the rate-determining step. Within this mechanism at fixed electrode potential, the OOH* formation barrier equals the OOH* adsorption free energy. The Fe site preferentially stabilizes OH* with incremental free-energy gain $\delta_{\mathrm{Fe}} \geq 0$ and the Ni site preferentially stabilizes O* with incremental free-energy gain $\delta_{\mathrm{Ni}} \geq 0$; when OOH* adsorbs bidentately, these two contributions are additive and independent. P doping, acting purely as an electronic modulator, increases $\delta_{\mathrm{Fe}}$ by $\Delta\delta_P \geq 0$ without affecting $\delta_{\mathrm{Ni}}$. The OH*–OOH* linear scaling relation constrains the base stabilization values by $\delta_{\mathrm{Ni}} = \alpha\,\delta_{\mathrm{Fe}}$ ($\alpha \geq 0$); $\Delta\delta_P$ is not subject to this constraint. The Faraday constant $F$ enters only through the overpotential and must not appear in any final symbolic answer.

Using these definitions, derive closed-form symbolic expressions — exclusively in terms of $\{\delta_{\mathrm{Fe}},\, \alpha,\, \Delta\delta_P,\, R_{\mathrm{target}}\}$ as appropriate — for the following three quantities and place all results in a single $\boxed{\cdots}$:

(a) $\dfrac{\partial R}{\partial \alpha}$, where $R$ is the ratio of the bidentate-plus-P-doping overpotential reduction to the bidentate-only overpotential reduction (both under the scaling constraint), holding $\delta_{\mathrm{Fe}}$ and $\Delta\delta_P$ fixed;

(b) $\dfrac{\partial(\Delta\delta_P^*)}{\partial\alpha}$ and $\dfrac{\partial(\Delta\delta_P^*)}{\partial\delta_{\mathrm{Fe}}}$, where $\Delta\delta_P^*$ is the P-doping increment at which the P-doped bidentate overpotential reduction equals exactly twice the un-doped bidentate overpotential reduction;

(c) the necessary and sufficient inequality on $\alpha$ (of the form $\alpha \leq f(\Delta\delta_P, R_{\mathrm{target}}, \delta_{\mathrm{Fe}})$) guaranteeing $R \geq R_{\mathrm{target}}$ for fixed $\Delta\delta_P > 0$ and $R_{\mathrm{target}} > 1$.

