In the Easley-O'Hara sequential trade model, the structural parameters are:
- $\alpha$: probability that an information event occurs in a given trading period;
- $\mu$: arrival rate of informed traders conditional on an information event;
- $\varepsilon$: arrival rate of uninformed (liquidity) traders on each side of the market (buy and sell independently);
- $\delta$: probability that the information event is bad news;
- $V_H$: asset value if the information event is good news;
- $V_L$: asset value if the information event is bad news ($V_H > V_L$);
- $\bar{V} = (1-\delta)V_H + \delta V_L$: prior unconditional expected asset value.

A risk-neutral market maker sets an ask price $A$ and a bid price $B$ to break even in expectation against informed and uninformed traders, so that
$$A = E[V \mid \text{buyer arrives}], \qquad B = E[V \mid \text{seller arrives}].$$

